<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Potnia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Potnia, meaning 'Mistress' or 'Lady', is a title of a goddess or goddesses recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> tablets of Mycenaean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a>, Pylos and other palaces, often with an epithet such as 'Potnia of the labyrinth' or 'Potnia of the horses'. She received offerings of oil, honey and other goods, and the word survived as an epithet of Greek goddesses.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/potnia.jpg" alt="Potnia" width="320" height="485" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lady_of_Auxerre_Louvre_Ma3098_n2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Potnia was a poetic title of honour, used chiefly in addressing women and goddesses; its masculine counterpart is posis. It goes back to a reconstructed Proto-Indo-European *pot-niha-, mistress, lady or wife, the feminine of *pótis, husband, and is related to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> pátnī-, lady or wife. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> it is written po-ti-ni-ja. A related word is despoina, mistress of the house, and one proposed etymology derives the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demeter">Demeter</a> from the same family of words.</p><p>A goddess of nature, birth and death was dominant in Bronze Age Minoan and Mycenaean cult, and among the Mycenaeans she bore the title Potnia. The earliest references to the title are inscriptions on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> tablets from Pylos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> dated 1450-1300 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Several Pylos tablets name po-ti-ni-ja alone, and the scholar Chadwick suggested that she was the mother-goddess of the Mycenaeans, with an important shrine at Pakijanes near Pylos. Her male companion was the wanax, a title usually given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a>. Elsewhere the title carries an epithet: a-si-wi-ja, possibly an Asian or Lydian woman; si-to-po-ti-ni-ja, of the grain, probably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demeter">Demeter</a> or her predecessor; i-qe-ja, the horse goddess; and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> a-ta-na-po-ti-ni-ja, Potnia Athana. A Minoan seal found by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-evans">Arthur Evans</a> shows a goddess with a spear on a mountain between rampant lions. The title may translate a Pre-Greek one, perhaps a-sa-sa-ra of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-a">Linear A</a> texts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> the title was given chiefly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demeter">Demeter</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persephone">Persephone</a>, and also to the earth goddess Gaia. Homer's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> names a potnia theron, mistress of the animals, who is Artemis. In Arcadian cult the nameless goddess of the mysteries was called Despoina, and Pausanias records that at Olympia Demeter and Persephone were called the Despoinai. The Eleusinian cult kept the pairing of the goddess and her companion in the words spoken there, &quot;Mighty Potnia has born a strong son&quot;. Karl Kerenyi identified Persephone with the mistress of the labyrinth.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Potnia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Linear B tablets written at Knossos after Greek speakers took over record offerings to a “<b>Potnia</b> of the labyrinth” and to Eleuthia at Amnisos, names that may preserve older Cretan cults.</blockquote><a href="/culture/minoan-civilisation">Minoan civilisation · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>The most frequent title is <b>Potnia</b>, “Lady” or “Mistress”, given to one or more goddesses, as in the “Potnia of the labyrinth” at Knossos and the “Potnia of the horses” at Pylos, and a “Potnia of Atana” at Knossos may be an early form of Athena.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mycenaean-civilisation">Mycenaean civilisation · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>Its Linear B archive records taxation of some 16 districts, landholding, bronze-smiths, flax and perfumed oil production, rowers for the fleet and offerings to Poseidon, Zeus, Hera and the <b>Potnia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-pylos">Kingdom of Pylos</a></li><li><blockquote>Administration used Linear A. After c. 1450 BCE Greek-speaking rulers took control and kept records in Linear B, listing sheep, wool, textiles and offerings to gods such as <b>Potnia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/palace-state-of-knossos">Palace State of Knossos</a></li></ul></section>
